<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <title>Terms &amp; biometric data notice — Keepsora</title>
  <%- include('partials/head') %>
</head>
<body>
  <%- include('partials/nav') %>
  <main class="page terms-page">
    <h1>Terms &amp; biometric data notice</h1>
    <p class="muted small">
      This is a plain-English summary for a demo project, not a substitute for a lawyer-drafted
      agreement. Anyone deploying this for real events should have counsel review it — face
      recognition triggers specific legal obligations in a number of places (see below).
    </p>

    <h2>What this service does with faces</h2>
    <p>
      When an organizer uploads event photos, the browser detects faces in them and converts
      each one into a numeric "descriptor" — a biometric identifier, even though no photo of the
      face itself leaves the device unencoded. When a guest submits a selfie, the same happens to
      it, and the two sets of numbers are compared to find matching photos. These descriptors are
      stored so matching can happen again later.
    </p>

    <h2>Organizer responsibilities</h2>
    <ul class="terms-list">
      <li>You confirm you have the right to upload and share the photos you add to an event.</li>
      <li>You're responsible for letting your guests know, before they attend or before you
        upload their photos, that face recognition will be used to help people find their own
        photos — and for honoring any request to be excluded.</li>
      <li>You're responsible for how you use any password protection and for whom you share a
        gallery link with.</li>
      <li>Illegal content, harassment, or uploading someone's photos without a lawful basis to do
        so is not allowed and may result in your account being suspended (see Admin/misuse
        handling below).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why this matters legally</h2>
    <p>
      Processing biometric identifiers (which includes face descriptors like the ones this app
      generates) is specifically regulated in a number of places — for example Illinois'
      Biometric Information Privacy Act (BIPA), Texas' CUBI, Washington State's biometric privacy
      law, and the EU/UK GDPR, which classifies biometric data used for identification as a
      "special category" needing an explicit legal basis (typically consent) to process. Several
      of these carry real financial exposure: BIPA in particular allows private lawsuits with
      statutory damages per violation, and has produced large settlements against companies that
      didn't get consent right. <strong>If you plan to run this for real guests, get legal advice
      on consent and retention before you do</strong> — this notice is informational, not legal
      advice, and doesn't make the product compliant on its own.
    </p>

    <h2>Data retention &amp; deletion</h2>
    <p>
      Photos and face descriptors are kept for as long as the event exists. An organizer can
      delete individual photos at any time, which also deletes their associated face data. There
      is currently no automatic expiry — operators running this for real users should decide on
      and implement a retention period appropriate to their jurisdiction.
    </p>

    <h2>Reporting misuse</h2>
    <p>
      Any guest viewing an event gallery can report it — for example if their photo was included
      without consent, or the gallery contains inappropriate content — using the "Report this
      gallery" link at the bottom of the gallery page. Reports go to the site's administrators,
      who can suspend the responsible account or remove the event.
    </p>
  </main>
</body>
</html>
